      "published": "",
      "collected": "2026-10-02",
      "abstract": "Human–objectinteraction (HOI) detection is an important task in computer vision. Existing methods primarily rely on end-to-end deep learning paradigms with specifically designed architectures. With the rapid advancement of large language models (LLMs) and agent technologies, incorporating agent-based collaborative mechanisms into visual understanding tasks has emerged as a promising research direction. In this article, we propose AgentHOI, which, to the best of our knowledge, represents an early attempt to apply multi-agent debate mechanisms specifically to HOI detection. Our framework comprises six collaboratively operating agents: the Hypothesis Proposer (HP) generates initial interaction hypotheses based on vision-language models; the Visual Analysis Agent (VAA) extracts spatial relationships and contact evidence; the Semantic Expert Agent (SEA) leverages LLMs for commonsense reasoning; the Physics Auditor Agent (PAA) validates the physical plausibility of interactions; the Debate Moderator (DM) orchestrates multiround debates to facilitate viewpoint exchange; and the Consensus Arbiter (CA) synthesizes evidence from all parties to render final verdicts. Through this novel ”hypothesis-debate-consensus” paradigm, multiple agents analyze and reason about interaction hypotheses from visual, semantic, and physical perspectives. We conduct experiments on the HICO-DET benchmark and provide a detailed analysis of the framework’s characteristics and limitations. Code is available at https://drliuqi.github.io/",
