      "category": "via-citation",
      "published": "",
      "collected": "2026-09-13",
      "abstract": "LLM-based multi-agent systems demonstrate potential for medical reasoning through multi-deliberation, yet the specific interaction mechanisms that enable collaborative diagnostic improvement remain poorly understood. While existing approaches employ parallel reasoning or consensus-driven dialogue, whether agents must respond to peer critiques versus merely receive them has not been systematically investigated. We developed a three-agent system (Ophthalmologist, Optometrist, Glaucoma Specialist) for glaucoma diagnosis using GPT-3.5turbo and tested three deliberation conditions on 50 cases from the REFUGE-Multirater dataset: (1) independent reasoning with voting, (2) unidirectional critique where agents received feedback but could not respond, and (3) bidirectional response where agents both critiqued and responded to peers. Unidirectional critique generated substantial epistemic uncertainty without any accuracy improvement. Bidirectional response yielded statistically significant improvement from 72.0% to 84.0%, with 6 cases improving and zero harm, exceeding the best individual agent performance while maintaining perfect sensitivity across all glaucoma cases. These findings demonstrate that effective multi-deliberation requires bidirectional response capability, with design principles extending beyond glaucoma to other multispecialty diagnostic tasks.",
