      "published": "",
      "collected": "2026-09-14",
      "abstract": "Automated tender document auditing is a high-complexity and high-stakes legal document intelligence task, because the system must not only identify hidden compliance defects, but also rigorously compare document facts against explicit legal constraints and further verify whether the resulting conclusions remain valid across multiple chapters of a long and semi-structured document. Existing commercial products and prototype systems often rely on general-purpose large language models or plain semantic retrieval, which makes them prone to shallow understanding, unstable legal grounding, weak cross-chapter consistency checking, and limited audit traceability. To address these limitations, this paper proposes a multi-agent framework for deterministic legal document auditing. At the core of the framework is a multi-agent workflow oriented toward different auditing priorities, where chapter-level specialized review and rule-aware full-text auditing work together to combine local defect detection with global consistency verification; this workflow is further supported by a structured rule library and task-oriented hybrid retrieval planning that organize legal provisions, clauses, and document facts around candidate issues. In this way, retrieved evidence is not treated as loosely appended context only, but is organized into explicit comparison constraints for compliance judgment. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed framework achieves high issue coverage and robust cross-chapter defect detection, offering a practical path toward rigorous, traceable AI-assisted tender auditing.",
