      "collected": "2026-09-27",
      "abstract": "Large language model driven multi-agent collaboration has become an important paradigm for solving complex tasks, yet whether communication volume can predict collaborative performance remains insufficiently examined empirically. Based on the research collaboration environment of MultiAgentBench, this study uses DeepSeek-V3 as the underlying model and conducts a statistical analysis of 712 inter-agent messages generated across 100 tasks. Nine communication features are extracted from three dimensions, namely message length, participation structure, and interaction network topology. Correlation analysis, Bootstrap confidence intervals, machine learning regression, clustering analysis, completion rate stratified comparison, and communication efficiency tests are then combined to examine the relationship between communication features and task completion rate. The results show that, under the present experimental setting, communication volume related indicators do not exhibit a stable or significant association with task completion rate. The correlation coefficients are low and statistically insignificant, all Bootstrap intervals cross zero, and the cross validated R-squared values of both random forest and Lasso regression are negative. Tasks with communication and tasks without communication show nearly identical average completion rates, and the completion rates of the three identified communication patterns do not differ significantly. Further analysis indicates that the apparent strong correlation between communication efficiency and speaking concentration is mainly driven by the number of messages.",
