      "collected": "2026-09-27",
      "abstract": "On-device multi-agent systems (MASs) have attracted increasing attention for their potential to minimize response latency, protect user privacy, and maintain service availability. However, stringent hardware resource constraints pose significant challenges to achieving efficient and reliable autonomous execution. This paper investigates the key challenges and technologies for efficient and reliable on-device MASs, and explores a joint optimization framework tailored for resourceconstrained devices. First, we present a systematic three-layer architecture for on-device MASs, consisting of the multi-agent layer, the model layer, and the device layer. Then, we provide a comprehensive analysis of challenges and representative techniques for enhancing the efficiency and reliability across the three layers. Finally, as a preliminary exploration toward cross-layer optimization, we propose a framework combining MoE-based parameter offloading with real-time validation, demonstrating the feasibility of simultaneously improving MASs' efficiency and reliability on resource-constrained devices. Experimental results show that the proposed framework significantly reduces the memory usage and improves task success rates of on-device MASs, paving the way for more efficient and reliable on-device MASs.",
