      "category": "via-citation",
      "published": "",
      "collected": "2026-09-27",
      "abstract": "Large Language Model (LLM)-based multi-agent systems (MASs) have demonstrated remarkable capabilities for helping people solve complex problems. Yet multi-agent systems are highly prone to errors, which often lead to task failures and token waste, significantly degrading efficiency. Recently, some MASs have used validation to improve the reliability and reduce resource waste. By assessing the correctness of intermediate states and final results, validation can expose errors and guide MAS execution. However, most of the existing MAS validation methods are post-hoc, which validate the task correctness at the end of executions. These validation methods cannot determine errors in time, resulting in a large number of invalid and inefficient executions of MAS. Therefore, in this paper, we propose HiVal, a hierarchical real-time validation framework to enhance the efficiency of MAS. HiVal employs a dual-layer validation to validate errors in multi-agent systems. The inner layer validates each intermediate result of subtasks, and the outer layer validates the final result of the overall task. Once detecting an error from the dual-layer validation, HiVal immediately triggers termination or replanning strategies to adjust the execution path. Comprehensive evaluations across two real-world multi-agent systems on four benchmarks indicate that HiVal significantly improves validation accuracy by 5.3% to 21.8% while reducing the token cost of MAS by 44.1% to 55.8% compared to state-of-the-art post-hoc validation baselines. Our code is available at https://github.com/Hi-Val/HiVal.",
