      "collected": "2026-10-11",
      "abstract": "Large language models (LLMs) remain vulnerable to hallucination, brittle intermediate steps, and excessive test-time computation. Uncertainty-Aware Adaptive Debate (UAAD) combines normalized token entropy, answer-level self-consistency, and cross-agent divergence to localize risky steps; a scheduler selects agents, rounds, and rebuttal focus, while stable consensus terminates debate. Evaluation uses deterministic public-trace replay on 100-sample subsets of GSM8K, MATH, CommonsenseQA, and HotpotQA, calibrated to published GPT-3.5-Turbo-0301 budget curves. Under the specified reconstruction, UAAD is assigned gains of 3.0, 5.0, 3.0, and 4.0 percentage points over 18-query fixed debate, with 46.7% lower normalized token use, a 44.3% lower sequential-depth proxy, and stress-replay instability reduced from 28% to 20%. These are simulation outputs from reconstructed transitions, not verified live gains on current LLMs; they support UAAD as a testable budget-control hypothesis requiring live-model validation.",
